 "accuracy": [
  [
   "Direct units, not a proxy.",
   "Units are HRB's own count of tax returns prepared (assisted + DIY, worldwide) read from each 10-K, not revenue divided by an assumed price. That removes the biggest error in most learning-curve exercises, but the figure is still not a clean 'production unit':"
  ],
  [
   "The cost line is wider than production.",
   "Cost of revenues is consolidated: besides tax-professional wages it carries office occupancy, depreciation, bad debt on fees and Emerald Advance loans, and the costs of financial products (Emerald Card, Spruce) and small-business services (Block Advisors, Wave), whose output is not a tax return. Marketing and corporate overhead sit in SG&A and are excluded."
  ],
  [
   "Units mix two very different products.",
   "An assisted return (a tax professional, an office, roughly $220-$240 of fees in company offices) and a free DIY online return are each counted as one. A shift toward DIY lowers unit cost with no learning; a shift toward assisted raises it. Here the shift ran toward DIY (U.S. assisted returns 14.5M in FY2013 vs 11.3M in FY2026, both quoted from the 10-Ks), which should have pushed unit cost down, so mix does not explain the rise."
  ],
  [
   "Inflation.",
   "Nominal cost per return moved +59.9% from FY2013 to FY2026; in FY2026 dollars it moved +12.7%. CPI-U is a consumer price index, not an index of HRB's inputs (mostly seasonal wages and rent), so the real series is an approximation."
  ],
  [
   "Definition changes and timing.",
   "Return counts were restated for several years (e-file acceptance basis, extensions excluded, Free File dropped); COVID pushed part of the 2020 season into FY2021; the 2021 change of fiscal year leaves a two-month gap; FY2023-FY2026 units are rounded to 0.1 million. The sensitivity checks above show how much each moves b."
  ],
  [
   "Cumulative volume starts at the window, not at 1955.",
   "N counts only returns since FY2013. With flat annual volume (-10.3% from first to last year) the window's cumulative count grows almost linearly, so ln N rises steeply early and flattens late: a fit on it measures how unit cost trended over 14 years, not HRB's position on a 70-year experience curve. Adding the pre-window history (not published in any filing used here) would compress all 14 points into a small slice of ln N."
  ]
 ],
 "interpretation": [
  "Nominal fit: unit cost RISES 11.4% per doubling (a negative learning rate), R\u00b2 = 0.79 over n = 14 years; inflation-adjusted: unit cost RISES 1.3% per doubling (a negative learning rate), R\u00b2 = 0.05. By the rule used here (R\u00b2 above ~0.8 = real curve, below ~0.5 = none visible) the nominal fit is between the two thresholds (a moderate fit) and the real fit is no curve visible, but what the nominal line fits is cost per return rising over time, which is the opposite of a learning curve; once inflation is removed, almost nothing is left to fit.",
  "For comparison, manufacturing studies typically find learning rates of roughly 10-30% per doubling and labor-heavy services far less; HRB shows no positive rate at all: cost per return was $61.59 in FY2013 and $98.49 in FY2026 (+60% nominal, +13% real), while annual volume was flat and the mix moved toward cheaper DIY returns; the 95% interval for the real learning rate (-4.8% to +2.1%) does not rule out zero.",
  "The most likely reading is that HRB is a mature firm far out on its curve, where each extra million returns teaches little, so its cost per return follows wages, rent and its non-tax products rather than accumulated experience.",
  "For a small esthetician-focused bookkeeping and tax firm, this says HRB's scale does not come with a falling cost per return that a newcomer can never catch: its advantage is brand, office network and software, not a steep experience curve, so a small firm's own learning in its first few hundred clients (templates, intake, niche deductions) can matter more than the giant's volume."
 ],
